<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Forest</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Schwarzwald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Forest is a wooded mountain range in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley and the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its valleys were divided among the Zähringen, the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, the Fürstenberg, abbeys such as Saint Blasien and small imperial cities such as Offenburg and Gengenbach. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">Timber</a>, mining and later clockmaking sustained its economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-forest.jpg" alt="Black Forest" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MWolf89 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick_vom_Hohfelsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Black Forest is a large forested mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, bounded by the Rhine Valley to the west and south and close to the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. It is the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>. It is about 160 kilometres long and up to 50 kilometres wide, with an area of about 6,009 square kilometres, and its highest peak is the Feldberg at 1,493 metres. The gentle eastern slopes contrast with the steeply falling terrain in the west that drops into the Upper Rhine Graben. It consists of a cover of sandstone on a core of gneiss and granite, and it was covered by glaciers in the last glacial period, which left tarns such as the Mummelsee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the range was known as Abnoba mons, after the Celtic deity Abnoba, and in Roman times as Silva Marciana, from the Germanic marka, border; it probably marked the border area of the Marcomanni, a part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>. Apart from Roman settlements on the perimeter, such as the baths at Badenweiler, mines near Badenweiler and Sulzburg and the Roman road of the Kinzig valley, the colonisation was carried out not by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> but by the Alemanni. They settled the valleys first and then increasingly higher areas, so that by the end of the 10th century the first settlements stood in the red sandstone region. Rötenbach, for example, was first mentioned in 819.</p><p>The ore deposits supported mining, and for several centuries logs were rafted down the Enz, Kinzig, Murg, Nagold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. This trade boomed in the 18th century and led to large-scale clearances. Most of the long, straight pine logs went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> for shipbuilding and were called Dutchmen; used there as piles for houses, they support many historic buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. Rafting largely ended in the late 19th century with the railways and roads.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">Charcoal</a> burners, potash boilers and glassmakers also lived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>. Because the valleys were relatively inaccessible, industrialisation arrived late. In winter many farmers made wooden cuckoo clocks, which developed in the 19th century into a precision engineering and watch industry, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> state government founded a clockmaking school at Furtwangen in 1850.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Black Forest is mainly rural, with many scattered villages and a few large towns, and the main dialect is Alemannic. It is known for its farmhouses with sweeping half-hipped roofs, the Bollenhut headdress worn in villages of the Kinzig valley, wood carving, the cuckoo clock, Black Forest ham and the Black Forest gâteau. In the 19th and early 20th centuries its rural beauty attracted artists such as Hans Thoma. Tourism is now the primary industry, and several ruined military fortifications date back to the 17th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A council of burghers governed the town, whose economy rested on wine, timber, crafts and traffic along the Kinzig valley route through the <b>Black Forest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-gengenbach">Free Imperial City of Gengenbach</a></li><li><blockquote>Offenburg grew at the crossing of the Rhine valley road and the Kinzig valley into the <b>Black Forest</b>, under the dukes of Zähringen.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-offenburg">Free Imperial City of Offenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Founded around 1090 as a priory of the <b>Black Forest</b> abbey of Saint Blasien, Ochsenhausen became an independent abbey in 1391.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-ochsenhausen">Imperial Abbey of Ochsenhausen</a></li><li><blockquote>The margraves built lands in the upper Rhine plain and the northern <b>Black Forest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden">Margraviate of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Zähringen line died out in 1218, the counts of Urach inherited part of their lands in the Baar and the <b>Black Forest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-furstenberg">Principality of Fürstenberg</a></li></ul></section>
